<Base title="About — Tomás Campoy Rojo" description="About Tomás Campoy Rojo, a Data Engineer focused on cloud data platforms, analytics engineering, automation, lakehouse systems and production reliability.">
  <section class="page-intro"><div class="eyebrow">About</div><h1>I like building systems that make the next decision easier.</h1><p>I’m Tomás Campoy Rojo, a Data Engineer working across cloud data platforms, analytics engineering, automation and architecture.</p></section>
  <article class="article">
    <p class="kicker">My default mode is to reduce ambiguity: define the source of truth, make transformations reproducible, expose data quality, and give downstream users a stable contract.</p>
    <h2>What I do</h2>
    <p>I work on data engineering problems that sit between raw operational systems and the people or products that need reliable information: ingestion, transformation, orchestration, analytical datasets, automation, performance and platform design.</p>
    <p>My current professional work is at Universidad Católica San Antonio de Murcia (UCAM), where I work on institutional data and analytics systems. I also build public data-engineering projects and take on selected technical collaborations outside that context.</p>
    <h2>Technical focus</h2>
    <p>My core stack is Python, Polars, SQL, Airflow, dbt, Spark and Databricks, with cloud work across AWS, Azure and GCP. I’m especially interested in lakehouse architecture, CDC, streaming, orchestration, data quality, observability and the operational side of data platforms.</p>
    <h2>How I work</h2>
    <p>I prefer measurable outcomes to technology theatre: correctness before cleverness, explicit trade-offs, performance that can be benchmarked, systems that are easy to inspect when they fail, and documentation that explains why the architecture exists.</p>
    <h2>Certifications</h2>
    <ul><li>AWS Certified Data Engineer – Associate</li><li>Azure Databricks Platform Architect</li></ul>
    <h2>Open to</h2>
    <p>Interesting data-engineering roles, technical collaborations, consulting projects and conversations with teams building serious data infrastructure. The best fit is usually a problem with real constraints, measurable impact and room to own the engineering decisions.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
